<Base title="Make it yours — Special" description="How to use a Special design bundle in your web or Jetpack Compose project, or with your coding assistant.">
<section class="container page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">THE GETTING-STARTED GUIDE</span><h1>Good taste.<br/><em>Meet good tools.</em></h1><div class="page-intro-bottom"><p>You’ve found a feeling. Here’s how to<br/>bring it into your own project.</p><a href="/styles/" class="text-link">Find a style first <Icon name="arrow" size={18}/></a></div></section>
<div class="container guide-layout"><nav class="guide-nav" aria-label="Guide sections"><span class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</span><a href="#download">01 / Get your bundle</a><a href="#understand">02 / Know what’s inside</a><a href="#web">03 / Build for the web</a><a href="#compose">04 / Build for Android</a><a href="#assistant">05 / Work with an assistant</a><a href="#quality">06 / Keep the quality</a></nav><div class="guide-content"><section id="download"><span class="eyebrow">01 / START HERE</span><h2>Choose it. Play with it. Take it.</h2><p>Each style page has a live showcase and a versioned ZIP download. Try the controls, look at the surfaces, and see whether the motion fits your project. Then download and extract the bundle.</p><a class="button" href="/styles/">Explore the six styles <Icon name="arrow"/></a></section><section id="understand"><span class="eyebrow">02 / THE WHOLE SYSTEM</span><h2>More than a pretty reference.</h2><div class="guide-file-list"><div><code>SKILL.md</code><p>The design language: principles, platform setup and how to use the style.</p></div><div><code>tokens.json</code><p>The exact colours, type, spacing and surface values.</p></div><div><code>motion.json</code><p>Spring presets, timing and reduced-motion behaviour.</p></div><div><code>components/</code><p>Recipes for the surfaces and controls that give the style its identity.</p></div><div><code>platforms/</code><p>Working web examples, CSS, React and Jetpack Compose components.</p></div><div><code>checklist.md</code><p>A final quality check, paired with anti-patterns to avoid.</p></div></div></section><section id="web"><span class="eyebrow">03 / THE WEB</span><h2>Start with something that works.</h2><p>Open <code>platforms/web/example.html</code> through a local web server. It links to the supplied CSS and any local helpers. Keep those files together, then use the components and token values to create your own layout.</p><div class="code-block"><span>LOCAL PREVIEW · FROM THE EXTRACTED BUNDLE</span><pre><code>python3 -m http.server 8000</code></pre><button data-copy="python3 -m http.server 8000" aria-label="Copy local preview command"><Icon name="copy" size={17}/></button></div><p>Visit the local server’s <code>/platforms/web/example.html</code> path. For React, the bundled <code>.tsx</code> components provide a starting point; read <code>INSTALL.md</code> for the style’s setup. Import the style’s CSS once and keep its global selectors scoped away from other design systems.</p></section><section id="compose"><span class="eyebrow">04 / ANDROID</span><h2>Bring the feeling to Compose.</h2><p>Copy the Kotlin files in <code>platforms/compose/</code> into your app, update the package as needed, and follow <code>INSTALL.md</code>. Bundles target Android 12 and newer. Shader-based effects use Android 13 APIs where available, with fallbacks for older supported devices.</p><p>Use the supplied motion helpers so slow motion and reduced motion stay consistent. Check the exact requirements inside the bundle before integrating a component.</p></section><section id="assistant"><span class="eyebrow">05 / BUILD WITH INTENT</span><h2>Give your coding assistant the system.</h2><p>Attach the extracted bundle, ask it to read <code>SKILL.md</code>, and describe the screen you need. A useful brief includes the platform, viewport, content and interactions—not just the name of the style.</p><div class="prompt-card"><span class="eyebrow">A GOOD STARTING BRIEF</span><p>“Read this bundle’s SKILL.md, tokens, motion and component recipes. Build a responsive settings screen in this style. Reuse the provided components, keep text readable, include keyboard focus, and respect reduced motion. Review the checklist before finishing.”</p><button class="text-link" data-copy="Read this bundle’s SKILL.md, tokens, motion and component recipes. Build a responsive settings screen in this style. Reuse the provided components, keep text readable, include keyboard focus, and respect reduced motion. Review the checklist before finishing.">Copy the brief <Icon name="copy" size={17}/></button></div><p>Bundles include editor adapters where applicable. These are guidance files, not a guarantee of output quality. Review what gets generated.</p></section><section id="quality"><span class="eyebrow">06 / KEEP IT SPECIAL</span><h2>The details are the design.</h2><ul class="quality-list"><li><Icon name="check"/>Review the actual screen at phone and desktop widths.</li><li><Icon name="check"/>Check text contrast against the surface behind it.</li><li><Icon name="check"/>Try the keyboard and every important interaction.</li><li><Icon name="check"/>Test reduced motion and make sure content stays complete.</li><li><Icon name="check"/>Use the bundle checklist, and keep its exact token values.</li></ul><p>Special’s bundled reference implementations are the starting point. Generated examples still need review; a good-looking first frame doesn’t prove the whole interaction works.</p></section></div></div>
</Base>
